[Music]  foreign  [Music]  this is your Tech news briefing for  Thursday September 29th I'm Zoe Thomas  for The Wall Street Journal  earlier this month the crypto platform  ethereum underwent a major change the  long-awaited ethereum emerged changed  how transactions on the network are  processed with the goal of reducing the  platform's environmental impact  in the lead-up to the shift the price of  ether the cryptocurrency tied to the  ethereum network nearly doubled but  since then the value of ether has fallen  drastically  so what's behind the reversal and how  long can it last  joining us to discuss that is our crypto  reporter Paul Vina hi Paul thanks for  joining me hi Paul one of the big things  that happened with the ethereum merge is  that it switched from a verification  system called a proof of work model to a  proof-of-stake model can you start by  reminding us what the difference is  between those two systems so I mean in a  very brief nutshell  the proof of work system  required ethereum's miners the people  that actually do the validating of  transactions to expend a certain amount  of computing power in order to have the  privilege of being part of the network  and you can earn money on it is very  profitable if you can do it at scale but  that's basically what you do you have to  put out Computing energy as a cost of  doing business what they have switched  to is a system where you're staking  money as your cost of doing business of  being what they now call a validator and  you are a node on the network and you're  processing transactions and you can earn  a little bit of money from that and then  of course the the big advantage of that  is that it is far more environmentally  friendly ethereum's energy usage went  down by about 99 after this software  upgrade so that that was the primary  reason for doing it how did that affect  the miners those who were kind of  creating The Ether cryptocurrency  basically it wiped out their business  this model ethereum miners had a  business where they bought Hardware to  process transactions and then they  hadn't built an entire business around  that and overnight that business  disappeared so those miners could either  take their machines and try to find a  different proof of work  cryptocurrency that would allow them to  keep doing that they could Scuttle their  hardware and just give up or they could  go in onto ethereum in the new model of  staking and put some money up so their  business model was it was eviscerated  overnight and so what are those miners  doing now with the ethereum that they  have well one of the things you saw in  the lead up to this big software switch  was the balance of ethereum that these  miners were holding there was a big  surge it went up a lot because they were  trying to get in their Last Licks so  they were mining as much as possible  they're doing a lot of work they're  holding on to it and then right around  the time of it you could see them start  selling so the price had gone up in  anticipation of this they turned up the  volume so to speak so they could mine as  much ether as possible and they sold at  the top  so you have these big miners selling off  after the merge but apart from that have  there been any hiccups since it happened  there haven't been any major hiccups and  that's a good thing there really could  have been the issue now becomes and this  is really with any software upgrade  anywhere this happens is well was there  anything in the code that is a problem a  bug that we didn't know about and it's  happened in the past on other upgrades  it's you know there's a history of it  you know something else that people seem  to be looking out for are maybe some  regulatory issues you know since the  merge we've heard the Securities and  Exchange Commission chair Gary Gensler  say that now ethereum might fall under  the sec's regulatory overview can you  explain that and how ethereum is  reacting this was one of the  probably more unexpected developments so  look this has been a big issue among  government Regulators what are  cryptocurrencies how do we Define them  how do we characterize them and regulate  them and there's been a big question of  whether cryptocurrencies are securities  are they Commodities you know what are  they and there have been different  answers to that and the sec's answer for  a while they've codified it but they're  sort of shorthand has been well that  Bitcoin and ethereum ownership is so  diffuse of the network itself that they  actually do operate like more like  Commodities than Securities they are not  securities  so Gary Gensler came out and said you  know what after the software upgrade now  that the model is you put up money and  you get paid for it that actually to him  he said it that kind of sounds like  ethereum could now be a security  so that is going to be something that  people in the ethereum world are going  to have to just pay attention to and  keep an eye on  you know we're talking about the drop in  ether's price but the market has been  pretty volatile lately I mean is there  anything different going on here  well you know stocks have been following  bitcoin's been following all these  cryptocurrencies have been falling and  for a little while ethereum was in its  own bubble really because there was a  lot of drummed up excitement over this  software upgrade now that the software  upgrade has been implemented and it's  over now ethereum is sort of starting to  fall back into the general malaise of  the rest of the world of risk assets  all right that's our reporter Paul Vina  thanks for joining us Paul yep thanks  for having me on  and that's it for today's Tech news  briefing if you want more Tech stories  check out our website wsj.com  I'm Zoe Thomas for The Wall Street  Journal thanks for listening  [Music]  [Music]

